WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.460
In the TV show "Ted Lasso,"

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:07.080
this 26,000-seat stadium
is filled to the brim.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.171
But this shot --

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:11.346
it was created during
the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:00:11.346 --> 00:00:13.300
Now, pause and zoom in here.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.517
See this woman? She's not really here.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.290
In fact, she was shot on a
completely different continent.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.123
And this shot?

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:27.420
The majority of these
people aren't even real.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.820
The "Ted Lasso" team pushed the boundaries

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.210
on how they could safely
fill out the stands.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.730
And knowing all the different
ways these crowds were made

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.060
could change the way we see large crowds

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.993
in movies and TV forever.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.268
The largest crowd scene ever shot

00:00:43.268 --> 00:00:44.570
was for the movie "Gandhi,"

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.211
which holds the Guinness World Record

00:00:46.211 --> 00:00:47.961
for most film extras,

00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:49.392
with this funeral procession,

00:00:49.392 --> 00:00:52.860
reportedly made up of
300,000 real marchers.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.940
But directing this many
extras is expensive,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.720
not to mention a huge
logistical challenge.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.520
So movies have turned
to using fake people,

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.280
like this painted matte
backdrop in 1959's "Ben-Hur"

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.860
or these inflatable extras
in 2006's "Glory Road,"

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.390
which are an easy way to
give more real-life depth

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.970
to a fake crowd in the distance.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.320
But fake crowds also come
with their own limitations.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.240
They can save the crew money
and properly fill an arena,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.670
but they can't move or
interact on their own

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.080
like real people.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.068
So the "Ted Lasso" team decided to use

00:01:26.068 --> 00:01:28.947
a combination of real and fake people

00:01:28.947 --> 00:01:31.960
to make their crowds look
as realistic as possible.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.172
Barnstorm VFX had two big
problems to deal with.

00:01:35.172 --> 00:01:36.810
First, because of the pandemic,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.047
they couldn't fill the stands with

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:40.170
as many real people as
they normally would.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.850
Plus, they weren't permitted to film

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.490
on the field at the real
Selhurst Park Stadium,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.220
which the fictional Nelson
Road Stadium is based on.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.078
So, how did they get all these people

00:01:49.078 --> 00:01:51.628
to fill 26,000 seats?

00:01:51.628 --> 00:01:54.520
They constructed an entire CG stadium

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.330
and used a technique called plate extras.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.180
Filming real people the crew can direct

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.400
is best for capturing
distinct gestures up close,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.050
like this moment in "Ted Lasso"

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.590
where the fans lift their fingers

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.282
and shout a very specific
curse word at Ted.

00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:09.315
Crowd: Wanker!

00:02:09.315 --> 00:02:10.300
Coach Beard: Let's give it a shot.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.610
Narrator: Plate extras are filmed

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:12.850
in front of a green screen

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.890
and then digitally scattered
into the desired shots.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.280
The extras are filmed
in an array of costumes

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.630
while doing a wide range of movements.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.820
Lawson Deming: Oftentimes a set of actions

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.440
that sort of flows into one another,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.970
like cheers that flow into a boo,

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.840
standing up, sitting down.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.590
Narrator: A typical
plate shoot might involve

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.560
well over 100 extras,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.110
but because of lockdown rules,

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.780
Barnstorm could only use around 20 people,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.210
members of its own team in Los Angeles.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.690
But social distancing
didn't hurt the production.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.230
Plate extras aren't filmed
next to each other normally,

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.860
but further apart than they
would be in a real crowd.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.848
That way, they can be placed
more easily in the final shot.

00:02:48.848 --> 00:02:51.970
These plate extras were also
filmed from multiple angles.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.447
Deming: We shot head-on, front 45,

00:02:55.447 --> 00:02:58.180
and then a full profile angle,

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.630
because we knew that we weren't gonna

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.080
see people from behind.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.913
Narrator: In this scene,
the majority of extras

00:03:03.913 --> 00:03:05.850
from this angle were actually on set,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:07.000
but this guy here?

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.975
Another extra who was shot separately,

00:03:08.975 --> 00:03:10.490
because, according to Deming,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.430
the production team didn't like
these two empty seats here.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.446
So we did, like, the
smallest crowd addition

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:18.537
we've ever done. It was a high angle,

00:03:18.537 --> 00:03:20.237
so we couldn't just shoot someone head-on,

00:03:20.237 --> 00:03:21.295
on a green screen.

00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:23.050
I had to estimate the angle and say,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.000
oh, what lens do we
think this was shot on?

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.740
What do we think the camera height was?

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.290
Get up on a ladder, shoot down,

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.790
look at the shot, compare it.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.320
Narrator: Still, Deming couldn't shoot

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.400
as many plate extras as he had hoped,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.530
so they only made up a
small portion of the crowd.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.820
And substituting in green-screened people

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.282
is never as good as having people on set.

00:03:43.282 --> 00:03:44.620
So when that's possible,

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.515
a production might choose a
technique called crowd tiling.

00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:49.870
Basically, the crew shoots one small group

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.110
and physically moves them around the set.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.690
They're then combined
together in postproduction.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.570
This is how the "Ted Lasso" crew created

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.650
part of the backdrop behind Jamie here.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.741
You can also see an
example of this technique

00:04:00.741 --> 00:04:02.966
in "Insecure," where Issa and her friends

00:04:02.966 --> 00:04:05.974
go to a concert in season
three, episode five.

00:04:05.974 --> 00:04:07.510
Take a look at this overhead shot

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:09.360
of hundreds of concertgoers.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:10.820
In reality, there was a group

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.403
of fewer than 200 extras on the field.

00:04:13.403 --> 00:04:14.510
In five separate shots,

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.572
that group was placed in the left, right,

00:04:16.572 --> 00:04:18.195
center, and back of the venue

00:04:18.195 --> 00:04:20.937
and rotoscoped together into one shot.

00:04:20.937 --> 00:04:23.710
Crowd tiling is key if you're
dealing with varied lighting.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.180
As you can see in the finished shot,

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.360
the crowds in the front,
the middle, and the back

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.530
are all being hit with
different color lights.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.410
And a lot of work has to be
done to hide the duplication.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:35.160
Deming: Your eye is very
good at catching duplication.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.780
When you're not careful about
how you do crowd tiling,

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.630
you can literally end up with

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.360
the same people next to themselves.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.210
Narrator: These tiles
that formed this one shot

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.020
from "Insecure" took an hour
and a half total to shoot

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.570
and end up on screen for only one second.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.240
And this technique really only works

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.030
when you have stationary shots.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.410
Deming: You only have that one angle.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.262
You can't move the camera during it.

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:58.378
It literally just works for

00:04:58.378 --> 00:04:59.911
the one angle that you shot it from.

00:04:59.911 --> 00:05:01.567
Narrator: For "Ted
Lasso," the limited amount

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.342
of real extras, both on set

00:05:03.342 --> 00:05:04.870
and in front of a green screen,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.590
left the crew with few
head-on shots for tiling.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.640
So how did they manage to get the stands

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.010
to look so full in shots like this?

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:15.270
Digital doubles -- a method
that's less realistic up close

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.048
but can be used to fill
in gaps from a distance.

00:05:18.048 --> 00:05:20.897
Digital doubles made up an estimated 90%

00:05:20.897 --> 00:05:22.500
of the show's crowd shots.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.950
The less detailed doubles
came from a template,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.260
but productions can
create more detailed ones

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.080
by scanning real actors,

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.240
like Margot Robbie in "I, Tonya."

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.890
This technique is the most
versatile of them all.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.880
Doubles can be relit
and shot from any angle.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.270
Plus, you can make more unique characters.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.110
Deming knew the "Ted Lasso" games

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.090
were largely shot in the evening.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:45.230
This meant dressing the
doubles for cold weather

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.740
in CG scarfs, hats, and jackets.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.810
Because they're completely CGI characters,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:54.180
we can control everything
about their appearance.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.350
Narrator: Unlike matte extras
and inflatable dummies,

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.370
you can also make digital doubles move

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.320
and react to the action in the scene.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.750
In "Ted Lasso," different
sections of the crowd

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.080
sometimes needed to have
two reactions at once,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.260
since two teams would
be playing each other.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:10.400
So they used a crowd-simulator
software called Golaem.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.035
Here, the software is identifying 90%

00:06:13.035 --> 00:06:15.525
of the available seats in
this section of the stadium,

00:06:15.525 --> 00:06:17.890
filling it with angry front-facing fans

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:19.700
dressed in the home team's colors.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.720
The same programming that allows us

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.730
to assign them a particular
color shirt, for example,

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:28.470
also allows us to assign
them a particular reaction.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.303
Narrator: During this shot,

00:06:29.303 --> 00:06:31.310
where AFC Richmond scores a goal,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.110
any digital double wearing
the red and blue of that team

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.450
was programmed to cheer.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.160
Deming: Because we have this control,

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.850
the entire crowd simulation
essentially knows.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:43.340
We can categorize them
as being on this side.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.610
Narrator: It still needs to feel

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.101
like a real human movement,

00:06:46.101 --> 00:06:48.184
as opposed to a faked movement.

00:06:48.184 --> 00:06:51.200
So Barnstorm's animators
donned motion-capture suits

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.320
and fed every movement to the software.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.500
And the hardest action? Clapping.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.560
Two CG hands going in for a clap

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.860
will naturally want to
intersect with one another,

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.330
and the suit alone couldn't
capture everything.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:05.050
Deming: Oftentimes you'll
end up in this situation

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.750
where, just imagine trying to clap

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.091
without moving your wrists

00:07:08.091 --> 00:07:09.650
and without moving your fingers.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.750
You end up with people
doing this sort of like,

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.108
a seal, you know,

00:07:13.108 --> 00:07:16.610
like the seal clapping for their food.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:17.980
Narrator: So the animators also wore

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.700
separate mo-cap gloves,

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.330
a relatively recent
innovation in the industry,

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.220
to perfect the complex hand movements.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.390
The key to faking a realistic crowd

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:28.850
like they did in "Ted Lasso"

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:32.090
is to combine these techniques
into a cohesive whole.

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.490
Deming: I think my preferred
method of creating a crowd

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:36.040
is anything that works.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.470
You're not restricting
yourself to one method,

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.990
so the audience is never
gonna quite catch on,

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.064
even if they think they
know what you're doing.

00:07:43.064 --> 00:07:46.027
Animator: Oh, my God! That's
a soccer ball in a goal!

00:07:46.027 --> 00:07:48.110
I'm so fricking excited!

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:48.943
Yes!

